<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chen Gongbo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1892">1892</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chen Gongbo was a founding member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> who later joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> and followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-jingwei">Wang Jingwei</a> into collaboration with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. He served as a leading official of the Japanese-backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> government and became its acting head after Wang's death in 1944. He was executed for treason in 1946.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chen-gongbo.jpg" alt="Chen Gongbo" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Chen_Gongbo_cropped.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Chen Gongbo was born in Nanhai, Foshan, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>, to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakka-people">Hakka</a> peasant family that had migrated from Shanghang in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>; his father was an official in the Qing administration. As a student at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peking-university">Peking University</a> he took part in the May Fourth Movement and studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a> under Chen Duxiu. He attended the First Congress of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> in July 1921, but was expelled the next year for supporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-jiongming">Chen Jiongming</a> in the June 16 Incident of 1922. He then went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and took a master's degree in economics at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbia-university">Columbia University</a> in 1925.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> and headed its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Peasants and Workers under Liao Zhongkai. He belonged to the party's left with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-jingwei">Wang Jingwei</a>, with whom he formed a close political and personal bond. Although he did much in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a>'s Northern Expedition, he and Wang strongly opposed Chiang as he took dictatorial power, and Chen thought it particularly unfair that Chiang had displaced Wang through a military coup in 1926.</p><p>During a period of cooperation between Chiang and Wang, Chen served as Minister of Industry from 1932 to 1936, and some of the economic policies he helped set remained in use under various Chinese regimes until the 1970s. As director of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> branch he helped organise the government's evacuation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a> began. When Wang broke with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> and set up the collaborationist government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, Chen followed despite his initial opposition and became speaker of the Legislative Yuan. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> handed nominal rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> to the Nanjing government in November 1940, he was appointed its mayor. In mid-1944, when Wang went to Japan for medical treatment, Chen was left in charge of the government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At the end of the Second World War Chen fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. On 9 September 1945 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s representative, He Yingqin, asked Japan's representative, Yasuji Okamura, to hand him over for trial, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/occupation-of-japan">American occupation</a> forces granted the request, and Chen was escorted back to China on 3 October. At his trial he defended himself vigorously, insisting that he had refused to cooperate with the Japanese in several significant matters and had acted only out of loyalty to his friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-jingwei">Wang Jingwei</a>. Convicted and sentenced to death, he took his fate calmly, saying that &quot;soon I will be reunited with Wang Jingwei in the next world&quot;. He was shot by firing squad at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangsu">Jiangsu</a>, on 3 June 1946.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chen Gongbo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In practice Japanese armies controlled it, and Wang's officials, among them <b>Chen Gongbo</b> and Zhou Fohai, had limited room for action.</blockquote><a href="/state/wang-jingwei-regime">Wang Jingwei regime</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nanhai. Died in Wu County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.31,120.62&amp;year=1927" aria-label="Wu County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Chen Gongbo"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5045 4888h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5045 4888h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=31.31,120.62&amp;year=1927">What was here in 1927? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
